  11. Patents.

  A "contributor" is a copyright holder who authorizes use under this
License of either the Program or a work on which the Program is based.
The contributor's original version is called the "contributor's
version".

  A "contributor's essential patent claims" are all patent claims
owned or controlled by the contributor, whether already acquired or
hereafter acquired, that would be infringed by some manner, by making,
using, or selling the contributor's version, but do not include claims
that would be infringed only as a consequence of further modification
of the contributor's version.  For the purposes of this definition,
"control" includes the right to grant patent sublicenses in a manner
consistent with the terms of this License.

  Each contributor grants you a non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free
patent license under the contributor's essential patent claims, to
make, use, sell, offer for sale, import and otherwise, modify and
propagate the contents of the contributor's version.

  In the following three sections, a "patent license" is any express
agreement or commitment, however denominated, not to enforce a patent
(such as an express permission to practice a patent or covenant not
to sue for patent infringement).  To "grant" such a patent license to
a party means to make such an agreement or commitment not to enforce a
patent against that party.

  If you convey a covered work, knowingly relying on a patent license,
and the Corresponding Source of the work is not available for anyone
to copy, distribute, and convey by the terms of this License or by
the terms of a separate license agreement with the contributor who
controls the patent, then you must either (1) cause the Corresponding
Source to be so available, or (2) arrange to deprive yourself of the
benefit of the patent license for this particular work, or (3)
arrange to extend the patent license in a downstream manner.  "Knowingly
relying" means you have actual knowledge that, but for the patent
license, your conveying the covered work would infringe one or more
patent claims in the relevant jurisdiction.

  If, pursuant to or in connection with a single transaction or
arrangement, you convey a property (other than "intellectual property")
and the grantor of the property conveys a patent license that is
specific to the conveyed property, then the patent license extends
to the property that is the subject of the transaction or
arrangement.

  A patent license is "discriminatory" if it does not include within
the scope of the coverage the use of any of the following: (a) the
contents of the Program; (b) any work that is based on the Program
and that is conveyed or could be conveyed with the Program in a
single transaction or arrangement.  If you convey a covered work, a
patent license is not required to the extent that the patent license
applies to a covered work and is not discriminatory.

  Nothing in this License shall be construed as excluding or limiting
any implied license or other defenses to infringement that may
otherwise be available to you under applicable patent law.

